  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy low stratus and fog are in place across\neastern NC this morning. With gradual heating and dry air\nintrusion expecting skies to gradually clear over the next few\nhours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cold front has now pushed farther offshore this\nmorning and flow aloft has become increasingly northwesterly as\nstrong heat dome over the western and central CONUS amplifies\nthe flow pattern. This is promoting an elevated thermal gradient\norientated NW to SE across much of the mid-Atlantic and\nCarolinas. Shortwave energy passing along this boundary, in\njuxtaposition of moderate instability and ample moisture will \nsupport a risk of showers and thunderstorms developing in the\nafternoon and pushing southeast. Axis of most supportive\nkinematics is expected to be displaced to our west and north,\nand CAMs keep most activity here during the day, rendering most\nof eastern NC dry today.\n\nOf note, almost all guidance points to a semi-organized cluster\nof storms developing upstream in WV/VA in the late afternoon \nand quickly pushing SEward towards eastern NC overnight. An \norganized cluster in the forecast pre-storm environment would \nfavor a marginal damaging wind threat. If this were to \nmaterialize, it would most likely approach areas along Highway \n64 just before midnight. Activity would likely decay as it\npushes farther southeast with poor shear and waning instability. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Anomalous upper troughing is forecast to remain\nin place across the Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas through at least\nthe middle of the week. Within this regime, a convectively \nactive NW flow pattern appears likely, and this is supported by \ndeterministic, analog, and machine learning guidance. The signal\nremains strongest on Tuesday, particularly later in the day, \nand this is the day that the Storm Prediction Center has \noutlooked a portion of our area in an elevated (15% probability)\nof severe thunderstorms. Synoptically, the overlap of building \ninstability and unusually deep layer shear for this time of \nyear certainly appears favorable for severe thunderstorm \ndevelopment. EFI CAPE-shear values are in the 80-90th percentile\nfor this event, especially for inland portions of the state.\n\nIn addition to the potential convective hazards, there will \nalso be a risk of dangerous heat. This risk also looks to be \nfocused on Tuesday when dewpoints and air temperatures are \nforecast to be the highest, with heat indices of 100-105 \ndegrees.",
